  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday Night through next Monday)\nIssued at 1035 AM CST Mon Nov 23 2026\n\nThe next chance of rain will begin Tuesday night, with rain\nchances jumping to near 100 percent Wednesday afternoon through \nThanksgiving morning. A complex pattern is setting up with a \nsouthern stream low pressure system merging with a strong \nnorthern stream upper level low. These systems will come together\noverhead Wednesday and Thursday, then spin off quickly up the Ohio\nValley on Friday. The heaviest rainfall (several inches) with \nthis combined system will occur along the east coast where a \nstrong surface low will draw in substantial Atlantic moisture. \nModels are still a bit unsettled on rainfall amounts for Middle TN\nwith model average around 1 inch. But the development of an \ninverted surface trough under the strengthening upper level system\nwould suggest some bands of enhanced rainfall, perhaps exceeding \n2 inches in some areas. We are not expecting flooding rainfall, \nbut it is shaping up to be a chilly soaker for Thanksgiving.\nThankfully, we are not expecting any severe storms here or around\nthe region.\n\nThe main area of rain will taper off Thanksgiving afternoon, but\nsome scattered showers will linger Thursday night into early \nFriday. A few snowflakes cannot be ruled out in the Mid State during\nthis time, but we are not looking for accumulations or major \ntravel impacts. Again, the main travel impact for the Thanksgiving\nholiday will be rainy weather. No severe storms or major winter \nweather impacts are expected across the region.\n\nThe weekend looks dry and seasonably cool. Next week's extended\noutlook shows temps climbing above normal with near-normal\nprecipitation.",
